219: We resolve the minimality conjecture \cite[Conjecture~4.10]{RWY} about the basic ideals $J_v$:
220: \begin{theorem}\label{thm:main}
221: Given a bigrassmannian permutation $v=v_{r,s,t,n}\in S_n$ let
222: \begin{align*}
223: i&\coloneqq s-t+1,\\
224: j&\coloneqq r-t+1,\\
225: a&\coloneqq \min(n-r-i,r-j),\\
226: b&\coloneqq \min(i,j).
227: \end{align*}
228: Then the basic ideal $J_v$, thought of as an ideal of $H^*(\mathrm{Gr}_{r,n})\cong \Lambda/I_{r,n-r}$, is minimally generated by
229: \begin{equation}
230: \label{eqn:minimallist}
231: \{\overline{s}_{\mu} \mid i^j\subseteq \mu \subseteq (i^j,b^a)\}.
232: \end{equation}
233: \end{theorem}
234: That (\ref{eqn:minimallist}) generates $J_v$ is \cite[Theorem 4.8]{RWY}. The conjectural part, which we prove,
235: is the minimality claim, that is, no generator can be removed without changing the ideal.

270: Now, we return to addressing Theorem \ref{thm:main}. Fix $v_{r,s,t,n}\in S_n$, and assign $i,j,a,b$ as in Theorem \ref{thm:main}. To prove the minimality of (\ref{eqn:minimallist}), we must show that for any $\rho\subseteq b^a$, the ring $\overline{\Lambda}$ does not admit syzygies of the form
271: \begin{equation}
272: \label{eqn:goal-syzygy}
273: \overline{s}_{(i^j,\rho)}=\sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\\lambda\neq \rho}}g_{\lambda}\overline{s}_{(i^j,\lambda)}.
274: \end{equation}
275: There is one important technicality we dispense with immediately: if $i=n-r$, then $a=0$ and Theorem \ref{thm:main} is trivial. Thus we will assume that $i<n-r$, so $a\geq 1$.
276: Actually, we will prove the slightly stronger statement that no expression (\ref{eqn:goal-syzygy}) exists for any $n,r,i,j,a,b\geq 1$ with
277: \[r<n,\quad a+j\leq r,\quad a+i\leq n-r, \quad \mbox{and}\quad b\leq i,j. \]
278: Fix any such integers $(n,r,i,j,a,b)$. Suppose one can find $\rho\subseteq b^a$ and polynomials $g_{\lambda}\in \overline{\Lambda}$ such that (\ref{eqn:goal-syzygy}) holds.
279: Since the Schur functions are homogeneous, both $\Lambda$ and $I_{r,n-r}$ are graded by degree. The quotient $\overline{\Lambda}$ inherits this grading. Thus, we can compare the degree $N=|\rho|$ homogeneous components of both sides of (\ref{eqn:goal-syzygy}) and rearrange to obtain a homogeneous syzygy of the form
280: \begin{equation}
281: \label{eqn:syzygy}
282: \sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|=N}}\overline{s}_{(i^j,\lambda)}f_{\lambda}=\sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|<N }}f_{\lambda}\overline{s}_{(i^j,\lambda)} \quad\mbox{with $f_{\lambda}$ homogeneous and }\deg f_{\lambda} = N- |\lambda|.
283: \end{equation}
284: By homogeneity, the left-hand side of (\ref{eqn:syzygy}) is simply a $\mathbb{Z}$-linear combination (that is, each $f_{\lambda}\in {\mathbb Z}$). We ignore the left-hand side of (\ref{eqn:syzygy}) for now and instead focus on the right-hand side.
285: Since $f_{\lambda}\in \overline{\Lambda}$, we can expand in the Schur basis to obtain
286: \[f_{\lambda} = \sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}}A_{\lambda,\theta}\overline{s}_{\theta}. \]
287: Then
288: \begin{align*}
289: \sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|<N }}f_{\lambda}\overline{s}_{(i^j,\lambda)}
290: &=\sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|<N }}\sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}}A_{\lambda,\theta}\overline{s}_{(i^j,\lambda)}\overline{s}_\theta\\
291: &=\sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|<N }}\sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}}A_{\lambda,\theta}\sum_{\nu\subseteq (n-r)^r} c_{(i^j,\lambda),\theta}^{\nu}\, \overline{s}_{\nu}\\
292: &=\sum_{\nu\subseteq (n-r)^r}\biggr( \sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|<N }}\sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}} c_{(i^j,\lambda),\theta}^{\nu} \, A_{\lambda,\theta}\biggr) \overline{s}_{\nu}.
293: \end{align*}
294: Viewing the parameters $A_{\lambda,\theta}$ as indeterminates, a syzygy of the form (\ref{eqn:syzygy}) for some $n,r,i,j,a,b,N$ is equivalent to a solution of the simultaneous linear equations
295: \begin{equation}
296: \label{eqn:eqs}
297: \biggr\{ \sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|<N }}\sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}} c_{(i^j,\lambda),\theta}^{\nu} \, A_{\lambda,\theta}=\chi(\nu)\,\biggr| \,\nu\subseteq (n-r)^r \biggr\},
298: \end{equation}
299: where $\chi(\nu)=f_\rho$ when $\nu=(i^j,\rho)$ for some $\rho\subseteq b^a$ with $|\rho|=N$, and $\chi(\nu)=0$ otherwise.
300: The following definition records the assumptions on the parameters used in the preceding considerations.
301: \begin{definition}
302: Call an integer tuple $\varphi=(n,r,i,j,a,b,N)$ \emph{valid} if $n,r,i,j,a,b,N\geq 1$ with
303: \[r<n,\quad a+j\leq r,\quad a+i\leq n-r,\quad b\leq i,j,\quad\mbox{and}\quad N\leq ab. \]
304: \end{definition}
